  'html' => '<h1>Managing Repair Projects</h1>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Who this is for:</strong> Project Managers who oversee repair work and Crew Leads who manage on-site operations.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Why This Matters</h2>
<p>This is the operational core of the system — turning assessments into completed work. Everything before this point is preparation. Everything after is documentation. <strong>This is where the actual repair happens.</strong></p>
<p>A well-managed project means the applicant gets their home fixed, the crew does good work, and the funder sees results. A poorly managed project means delays, budget overruns, and a household stuck in limbo.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Create Work Orders from the Assessment</h2>
<p>Once the assessment is submitted and approved:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Open the case</strong> in your dashboard</li>
<li><strong>Review the assessment</strong> — what repairs are needed?</li>
<li><strong>Create a Project</strong> — give it a name (e.g., "42-Cherry: Roof &amp; Plumbing Repair")</li>
<li><strong>Break repairs into Repair Tasks</strong> — each task is one actionable item</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Task structure:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Task name</strong> — specific and actionable ("Replace damaged shingles on east roof section")</li>
<li><strong>Assigned trade</strong> — roofing, plumbing, electrical, etc.</li>
<li><strong>Assigned to</strong> — crew member, subcontractor, or volunteer team</li>
<li><strong>Estimated hours</strong> — from the assessment</li>
<li><strong>Materials needed</strong> — list what\'s required</li>
<li><strong>Priority</strong> — urgent, normal, or low</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> If two different people could work on it independently, it should be two separate tasks. "Fix the bathroom" is a project. "Replace subfloor," "Install new vanity," and "Repair shower surround" are tasks.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Assign Tasks</h2>
<h3>To Crew Members</h3>
<p>Assign tasks to your in-house crew when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The work is within their skill set</li>
<li>You have availability in their schedule</li>
<li>The task doesn\'t require specialized licensing (electrical, plumbing may need licensed subs)</li>
</ul>
<h3>To Subcontractors</h3>
<p>Assign to subcontractors when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The work requires a licensed professional</li>
<li>The task is outside your crew\'s capabilities</li>
<li>You need specialized equipment</li>
</ul>
<h3>To Volunteers</h3>
<p>Assign to volunteers when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The task is volunteer-friendly (demo, painting, cleanup)</li>
<li>Supervision is available (crew lead on-site)</li>
<li>Safety risks are minimal</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Important:</strong> Volunteers should never be assigned tasks requiring licensed work, working at heights, or handling hazardous materials.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Material Requests</h2>
<p>If tasks require materials:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Create a material request</strong> in the platform</li>
<li><strong>List items needed</strong> — be specific (not "lumber" but "2×4×8 pressure-treated, 12 pieces")</li>
<li><strong>Submit for approval</strong> — PM or admin approves the request</li>
<li><strong>Track delivery</strong> — note when materials arrive on-site</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Best practice:</strong> Order materials before the crew starts that task. Nothing kills momentum like "we can\'t do the work because the materials haven\'t arrived."</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Track Progress on the Dashboard</h2>
<p>Your dashboard should show:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tasks by status</strong> — pending, in progress, complete, blocked</li>
<li><strong>Tasks by assignee</strong> — who\'s working on what</li>
<li><strong>Budget status</strong> — how much has been spent vs. allocated</li>
<li><strong>Timeline</strong> — start date, estimated completion, actual progress</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Update tasks daily:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Mark tasks <strong>In Progress</strong> when work begins</li>
<li>Mark tasks <strong>Complete</strong> when finished</li>
<li>Add <strong>photos</strong> of completed work</li>
<li>Log <strong>hours worked</strong> — this matters for grant reporting and volunteer recognition</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>If you didn\'t log it, it didn\'t happen.</strong> Funders need to see hours, costs, and outcomes. Your dashboard data becomes their report.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Handle Change Orders</h2>
<p>Sometimes the scope changes. The crew opens a wall and finds mold. The assessment missed something. The applicant has an additional need.</p>
<p><strong>Change order process:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Document the change</strong> — what\'s different from the original assessment?</li>
<li><strong>Estimate the impact</strong> — additional cost, time, materials</li>
<li><strong>Get approval</strong> — PM or admin approves the change</li>
<li><strong>Update the project</strong> — add new tasks, adjust budget, revise timeline</li>
<li><strong>Notify the applicant</strong> — they should know what changed and why</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Never do unapproved extra work.</strong> If it\'s not in the project scope and hasn\'t been approved through a change order, it doesn\'t get done. This protects the organization, the funder, and the applicant.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 6: Coordinate Multiple Trades</h2>
<p>When multiple trades work on the same property:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Sequence matters</strong> — electricians before drywall, plumbers before flooring</li>
<li><strong>Communicate the schedule</strong> — everyone should know when they\'re on-site</li>
<li><strong>Designate a site lead</strong> — someone on-site to coordinate (usually the crew lead)</li>
<li><strong>Log handoffs</strong> — when one trade finishes and another begins, note it</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Common sequencing:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Emergency/stabilization first</li>
<li>Structural repairs</li>
<li>Roof and exterior envelope</li>
<li>Plumbing and electrical rough-in</li>
<li>Insulation and drywall</li>
<li>Finish work (painting, flooring, fixtures)</li>
<li>Final inspection and cleanup</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Step 7: Quality Control Checkpoints</h2>
<p>At key milestones, verify the work:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Checkpoint</th>
<th>What to Verify</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>After demo</strong></td>
<td>All damaged material removed, structural issues visible</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>After rough-in</strong></td>
<td>Plumbing/electrical/HVAC installed correctly before walls close</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>After finish</strong></td>
<td>Work meets quality standards, no visible defects</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Final walkthrough</strong></td>
<td>Everything in scope is complete, site is clean, applicant is satisfied</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>If something doesn\'t pass:</strong> Note the issue, assign a corrective task, and re-inspect when complete. Don\'t close a task that isn\'t actually done.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 8: Mark Tasks Complete and Close Work Orders</h2>
<p>When all tasks in a project are complete:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Verify each task</strong> — photos uploaded, hours logged, materials recorded</li>
<li><strong>Do a final walkthrough</strong> — with the applicant if possible</li>
<li><strong>Mark the project Complete</strong> in the platform</li>
<li><strong>Log the completion event</strong> — "Project [name] completed on [date]"</li>
<li><strong>Trigger the closeout process</strong> — invoice reconciliation, after photos, applicant sign-off</li>
</ol>
